<PageLayout
  title="Cooked Score Methodology | Model Version 2.0.0"
  description="The complete Cooked Score 2.0.0 methodology: seven inputs, four component formulas, weights, scoring anchors, assumptions, limitations, and change log."
  path="/money-tools/financial-health-check/methodology/"
  eyebrow="Transparency"
  heading="How the Cooked Score is calculated."
  lead="Every part of model version 2.0.0 is documented here so you can understand what the number includes—and what it leaves out."
  updated="August 17, 2026"
>
  <ContentNote title="Educational model, not a professional standard" tone="caution">
    <p>
      Cooked Finance created this model for general education. Its weights and
      scoring curves are editorial product choices; they have not been
      validated as a professional financial-health standard. The result is not
      financial, investment, tax, credit, or legal advice.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <h2>Model at a glance</h2>
  <p>
    Version 2.0.0 uses seven user inputs to calculate four component scores from
    0 to 100. The components are combined as follows:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cooked Score = round((cash buffer × 0.30) + (payment DTI × 0.25) + (savings habit × 0.20) + (retirement progress × 0.25))</strong>
  </p>
  <EvidenceTable caption="Cooked Score 2.0.0 component weights">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">Primary question</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Cash buffer</th><td>30%</td><td>How long could liquid cash cover essential spending?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Monthly payment DTI</th><td>25%</td><td>How much gross income is committed to required debt payments?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Savings habit</th><td>20%</td><td>How much gross income is regularly being set aside?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Long-term retirement progress</th><td>25%</td><td>How does the retirement balance compare with an age-based planning guidepost?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </EvidenceTable>

  <h2>Inputs and definitions</h2>
  <EvidenceTable caption="The seven inputs used by model version 2.0.0">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Definition used in the model</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Age</th><td>One adult’s age in completed years; used only for that person’s long-term guidepost.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Gross annual income</th><td>That adult’s personal income before taxes and payroll deductions. Do not combine partners; run a separate check for each adult.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Essential monthly spending</th><td>Recurring needs such as housing, basic utilities, groceries, insurance, transport, health care, and required minimum payments.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Liquid cash</th><td>Cash available without selling investments: checking, savings, and similar cash accounts. Retirement accounts are excluded.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Monthly debt payments</th><td>Required monthly payments on mortgages, rent-to-own debt, cards, student loans, vehicle loans, personal loans, and other debt obligations.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Monthly saving</th><td>Recurring contributions to cash savings, retirement accounts, and long-term investments, including employee contributions but excluding market gains.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Retirement assets</th><td>Vested account balances intended for retirement or long-term investing. A current vested cash-balance pension value may be included when it appears as an account balance; projected monthly or annual defined-benefit pension income is excluded. Do not include a primary home or emergency cash.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </EvidenceTable>

  <h2>Component formulas</h2>

  <h3>1. Cash buffer</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Buffer months = liquid cash ÷ essential monthly spending.</strong>
    The model linearly interpolates between 0 months = 0 points, 0.5 month = 15,
    1 month = 30, 2 months = 55, 3 months = 75, 4.5 months = 90, and
    6 months = 100. Values above 6 months remain at 100. The broader 3–6 month
    planning context is described in
    <a href="https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/smart-money/emergency-fund" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Fidelity’s emergency-fund guide<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>;
    the component curve and its point values are Cooked Finance modeling choices.
    This is a resilience indicator, not a command to hold exactly six months.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Monthly payment debt-to-income ratio</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Gross monthly income = gross annual income ÷ 12.</strong><br />
    <strong>Payment DTI = required monthly debt payments ÷ gross monthly income.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    This follows the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau’s definition of DTI
    as monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly income. Different lenders
    use different limits, so Cooked Finance does not label a particular ratio
    as an approval threshold. See the
    <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-debt-to-income-ratio-en-1791/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB’s DTI explanation<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scoring anchors are 0% = 100 points, 10% = 90, 20% = 75, 30% = 60,
    36% = 50, 43% = 35, 50% = 20, 60% = 5, and 75% or more = 0.
    Values between anchors are linearly interpolated.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Savings habit</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Savings rate = recurring monthly saving ÷ gross monthly income.</strong>
    Scoring anchors are 0% or less = 0 points, 2% = 15, 5% = 35, 10% = 65,
    15% = 90, and 20% or more = 100. Employer contributions are omitted unless
    the user deliberately includes them in the monthly-saving input.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Long-term retirement progress</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Retirement multiple = retirement assets ÷ gross annual income.</strong><br />
    <strong>Progress ratio = retirement multiple ÷ age-based target multiple.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    The target curve uses published Fidelity planning guideposts of 1× income by
    age 30, 3× by 40, 6× by 50, 8× by 60, and 10× by 67. Fidelity states that
    these guideposts assume, among other things, saving 15% from age 25,
    retiring at 67, and seeking to maintain a pre-retirement lifestyle. They
    are starting points, not universal requirements. Read
    <a href="https://www.fidelity.com/viewpoints/retirement/how-much-do-i-need-to-retire" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Fidelity’s assumptions and guide<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cooked Finance linearly interpolates between the published guideposts. For
    younger users, version 2.0.0 uses a proprietary 0.1× floor from ages 18
    through 25, then rises linearly from 0.1× at 25 to Fidelity’s published 1×
    guidepost at 30. At age 67 and above, the target remains 10×. The early-career
    floor and all interpolation are model choices, not Fidelity recommendations.
    Progress scoring anchors are 0% of target = 0 points, 10% = 10, 25% = 30,
    50% = 55, 75% = 78, and 100% or more = 100.
  </p>

  <h2>Input rules and rounding</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The public form accepts ages 18–80. The engine itself normalizes ages to whole years and clamps programmatic inputs to 18–100.</li>
    <li>Money inputs are normalized to finite, non-negative values with two decimal places. Missing, invalid, or negative programmatic values become zero.</li>
    <li>The public check requires annual income and essential monthly spending greater than zero because those values are ratio denominators.</li>
    <li>For defensive programmatic use, zero annual income makes the three income-based components unavailable, while zero essential spending makes the cash-buffer component unavailable; those components receive zero points and trigger warnings.</li>
    <li>Each interpolated component is rounded to a whole-number 0–100 score. Weighted contributions are rounded to two decimal places, then their sum is rounded to the final whole-number score.</li>
    <li>Component values are capped from 0 through 100, so one unusually strong input cannot erase all other risks.</li>
    <li>All money inputs must use the same currency. Currency choice does not affect ratios.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the model deliberately does not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not estimate a population percentile or “financial age.”</li>
    <li>It does not use Federal Reserve wealth tables to award or remove score points.</li>
    <li>It does not distinguish high-interest debt from low-interest debt.</li>
    <li>It does not value a home, projected defined-benefit pension income, business, insurance policy, or expected government benefit.</li>
    <li>It does not project investment returns, inflation, taxes, retirement spending, or life expectancy.</li>
    <li>It is designed for one adult at a time and does not adjust for household size, a partner’s age or finances, local costs, disability, unstable income, or caregiving.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why those limitations matter</h2>
  <p>
    Two people with the same inputs may reasonably make different choices. A
    person with highly variable income may need more cash than the model rewards.
    Someone with secure defined-benefit pension income may need less invested wealth than the guidepost
    suggests. A mortgage payment may increase DTI while the related home equity is
    absent from the score. Treat the output as a prompt for questions, not a plan.
  </p>

